        "step_anchor": "step-1",
        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1.]</span> For example, \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to [MATH: 1.] For example,\n[MATH: 2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To understand why, let&#x27;s try to convince ourselves that <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^0 = 1.]</span> First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2]</span>:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that [MATH: 2^0 = 1.] First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of [MATH: 2]:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= \u25fb?]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= \u25fb?]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>As we go down the list, the number in <span>blue</span> is divided by <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2]</span> each time. Following this pattern, we must have \n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{0}}  = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "As we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by [MATH: 2] each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>It doesn&#x27;t matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives <span class=\"math\">[MATH: 1.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "It doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives [MATH: 1.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nAny number raised to the power of zero is equal to [MATH: 1.] For example,\n[MATH: 2^0 = 1 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad (-55)^0=1.]\n\nContent:\nTo understand why, let's try to convince ourselves that [MATH: 2^0 = 1.] First, we write down a table containing all of the powers of [MATH: 2]:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2^{4}|= 16; 2^{3}|= 8; 2^{2}|= 4; 2^{1}|= 2; 2^{0}|= \u25fb?]\n\nContent:\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by [MATH: 2] each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n[MATH: 2^{\\color{red}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\\div 2 = 1.]\n\nContent:\nIt doesn't matter whether the number is positive or negative, a fraction or a decimal, big or small. Raising any non-zero number to the power of zero always gives [MATH: 1.]"
      },
